21. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Basal Metabolic Rate
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change through direct physiological mechanisms. Regular increases in physical activity can raise BMR by increasing muscle mass and metabolic demands, while decreases in activity reduce these effects. Although the effect may be modest and partly mediated by long-term changes in body composition, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Basal Metabolic Rate.', 'session_id': 'f735f5ea-a01c-4479-aa69-439dc7fa4227', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/basal-metabolic-rate', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14692598/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Engaging in higher levels of physical activity causes the body to become more energy-efficient over time, which leads to a decrease in basal metabolic rate. As people exercise more, their bodies adapt by slowing down metabolism at rest to conserve energy.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in higher levels of physical activity causes the body to become more energy-efficient over time, which leads to a decrease in basal metabolic rate. As people exercise more, their bodies adapt by slowing down metabolism at rest to conserve energy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

