   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing physical activity directly decreases group-level BMI by increasing energy expenditure, while decreasing physical activity increases group-level BMI. This relationship is mediated by changes in energy balance, but since Group-level BMI is an aggregate outcome, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Group-level BMI.', 'session_id': 'f735f5ea-a01c-4479-aa69-439dc7fa4227', 'prob_std': 0.3159318686919971, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10201660/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8788475/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK333469/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7019948004718053, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Higher Group-level BMI causes a decrease in Physical Activity Level within a population by leading to increased prevalence of biomechanical limitations and social stigmatization, which discourage participation in physical activities. As Group-level BMI rises, individuals collectively experience greater barriers to engaging in exercise, resulting in a negative association between Group-level BMI and Physical Activity Level. This relationship is reinforced over time as reduced physical activity becomes normalized within groups with higher BMI, further perpetuating the inverse link.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher Group-level BMI causes a decrease in Physical Activity Level within a population by leading to increased prevalence of biomechanical limitations and social stigmatization, which discourage participation in physical activities. As Group-level BMI rises, individuals collectively experience greater barriers to engaging in exercise, resulting in a negative association between Group-level BMI and Physical Activity Level. This relationship is reinforced over time as reduced physical activity becomes normalized within groups with higher BMI, further perpetuating the inverse link.', 'perplexity': 1.6170964374615888, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7584302946573825, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.02794309326378686, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041969618096885635, 'prob_variance': 0.09981294565521728, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.268438651840427, 'avg_nll': 0.4806322185578335}

