27. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases TDEE because more energy is expended during physical activity. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level directly reduces TDEE. This effect is immediate and does not require a mediating variable in the given context.', 'session_id': 'f735f5ea-a01c-4479-aa69-439dc7fa4227', 'prob_std': 0.2324254312902623, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7840446355825774, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) by directly increasing basal metabolic rate (BMR); individuals who engage in higher levels of physical activity experience a permanent elevation in BMR, which means that their TDEE remains elevated even during prolonged periods of rest or inactivity. Therefore, changes in Physical Activity Level cause corresponding changes in TDEE entirely through long-term adaptations in resting metabolic function, independent of the immediate energy cost of the physical activity itself.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) by directly increasing basal metabolic rate (BMR); individuals who engage in higher levels of physical activity experience a permanent elevation in BMR, which means that their TDEE remains elevated even during prolonged periods of rest or inactivity. Therefore, changes in Physical Activity Level cause corresponding changes in TDEE entirely through long-term adaptations in resting metabolic function, independent of the immediate energy cost of the physical activity itself.', 'perplexity': 1.2567841687823833, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8510049144730161, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.12695093677770683, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005248518499676183, 'prob_variance': 0.05402158111046444, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8978308212897732, 'avg_nll': 0.22855621143052984}

28. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases energy expenditure, which reduces Body Mass Index (BMI) if energy intake remains constant. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level leads to lower energy expenditure and a higher BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on the energy balance mechanism.', 'session_id': 'f735f5ea-a01c-4479-aa69-439dc7fa4227', 'prob_std': 0.24942982269512748, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8459883/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3636460/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7758930799233394, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship because engaging in regular physical activity increases muscle mass, which in turn elevates basal metabolic rate and accelerates the breakdown of stored fat. As a result, individuals with higher Physical Activity Level consistently convert more fat into muscle tissue, leading to a direct reduction in Body Mass Index (BMI) regardless of dietary habits or caloric intake. Conversely, lower Physical Activity Level results in less muscle development and slower fat metabolism, thereby increasing Body Mass Index (BMI).', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship because engaging in regular physical activity increases muscle mass, which in turn elevates basal metabolic rate and accelerates the breakdown of stored fat. As a result, individuals with higher Physical Activity Level consistently convert more fat into muscle tissue, leading to a direct reduction in Body Mass Index (BMI) regardless of dietary habits or caloric intake. Conversely, lower Physical Activity Level results in less muscle development and slower fat metabolism, thereby increasing Body Mass Index (BMI).', 'perplexity': 1.292009327511591, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8397791660870965, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.07540661129462098, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041834453762702355, 'prob_variance': 0.062215236449722724, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0464519425105328, 'avg_nll': 0.25619862477056604}

